 { We will split our study on semi-open files into two parts. First, we'll look at backward pawns. Then, we'll move on to applying (and defending against) piece pressure where we'll cover some situations that arise often. Our first example on backward pawns is taken from the game Chajes,O - Capablanca,J (1915) . The most important positional feature is that Black has a powerful semi-open d-file, leaving him with active rooks while White has to defend passively. Let's see how Capablanca tried to make progress. } 26... { -KEY- } b6 { The backward pawn on d4 leaves White on the defensive, so Black goes for a pawn break to put the pressure on. } 27. cxb6 $2 { Chajes mistakenly changes the structure, with close to fatal consequences. } ( { White needed to play } 27. b4 $11 { when he would have been passive, but holding everything together as the rook on d3 does a great job of defending the vulnerable a3 pawn. } ) 
axb6 $1 { Now the ...c6-c5 pawn advance becomes inevitable, and there is no configuration of heavy pieces that can prevent the deadly pin on the d4 pawn from leading to the eventual loss of Black's d-pawn. } 28. Rg3 { White stakes his future on an attack, but the absence of minor pieces coupled with Black's intact kingside pawn cover renders the plan ineffective. } c5 29. Qh4 $2 { White goes for one last trick. } ( 29. Qf4 { This move was better, creating some tactical resources based around moves such as Qf4-f6 and h4-h5-h6. } ) 
g6 $1 { Killing off any attacking possibilities. The pawn on d4 is not running anywhere. } ( 29... Rxd4 $4 { Black would have had a sleepless night if he fell for White's desperate trick. } 30. Qxd8 $1 Rxd8 31. Rxd8 ) 
30. Rf1 $4 { This blunder ended the game prematurely, though White's position was already lost. } ( 30. Rgd3 { was the best practical try, but here Black would pressure the d4 pawn with the queen sooner or later, leading to a loss of the vital d4 pawn. For example: } R8d7 31. Qe4 Qa4 $19 ) 
Qxf1 { White resigned. } 
