 { Our second example is also taken from a famous classic game - Botvinnik,M -Capablanca,J (1936) . White has what is known as a Maroczy Bind (pawns on e4 and c4 creating a vice-like grip on the d5 square) . As a result, Black's pawn on d7 finds it very difficult to advance beyond the d6 square. The broad plan here that has been discovered in times after Capablanca is the so-called 'Hedgehog formation' where Black places their pawns on e6, d6, b6, and a6 (forming a row of 'spines', hence the name) . However, at this time the correct treatment was not known and so Black was forced to think independently. He quickly goes wrong... } 16. { -KEY- } Rd2 { White builds up his forces along the semi-open file. } f5 $6 { Black has the right idea of undermining the centre, but the wrong execution. The queenside pawn break with ...a6 and ...b5 would have been a better approach since this would not have weakened either the kingside or the e5 square. } ( 16... a6 $1 { This is the modern approach. } 17. Rfd1 b5 $1 18. cxb5 axb5 { By exchanging off White's c4 pawn, it becomes more likely that in the future Black could advance their d-pawn to d5, in this way getting rid of the backward pawn. } ) 
17. Rfd1 Bg5 18. Rd3 { The pressure against the d7 pawn is already very uncomfortable. } Bf6 { Black provokes the advance of the e-pawn in order to shut down the a1-h8 diagonal, and at at the same time soften up the h1-a8 diagonal. } 19. e5 $1 { Nevertheless, the price to pay is too high. White is inching closer to a complete bind on the d7 pawn. } Be7 20. Qf2 $1 { Stepping the queen back, since the rooks are more effective at pressuring the d7 pawn. The pawn on d7 is now a backward pawn, which is defined as 'a pawn that is behind all pawns of the same color on the adjacent files, and cannot be safely advanced.' } Rf7 ( 20... d6 $2 { Black cannot untangle. } 21. Ba3 $18 { The pawn will fall, since ...dxe5 is met by Rxd8, winning material. } ) 
21. Qd2 ( { Tripling up on the d-file once again. The threat is } 21. Qd2 -- 22. Rxd7 { . } ) 
Bb4 22. a3 Bf8 { Provoking the move 22.a3 has not changed matters. The problem remains the pawn on d7! Now Botvinnik sets about the task of improving his position further. Notice the paralysing effect that the vulnerable d-pawn has had on Black's game. Every piece is tied down in an effort to keep it from falling. } 23. Ne2 $1 { The knight was not doing much on c3, so Botvinnik goes about improving it. } Nc7 24. Nf4 g6 25. h4 $1 { With the knight firmly anchored on the f4 square, ideas like Nxe6 or even h4-h5 at the right moment are problematic. } b5 { Out of frustration, Black takes active measures. However, this only worsens matters as there are now more open lines of attack for White (the c-file, and the a5-d8 diagonal come to mind). } 26. cxb5 Qxb5 27. Rc1 $1 { Shifting his attention to the newly opened file. } Qb7 28. Rxc7 $6 { Botvinnik spots a way of winning a pawn and is unable to resist. It is understandable. Experienced players understand that 'tactics flow from a superior position' and so White had likely been on the lookout for a way of forcing a breakthrough for some time. } ( { However, he overlooked a much cleaner solution in the form of } 28. Qa5 $1 { The knight cannot move without losing the rook on d8. } Rc8 ( 28. Rxc7 d5 29. exd6 Bxd6 30. Be5 { is also hopeless. } ) 
29. Rdc3 d5 30. Nxe6 $18 { Black loses an exchange and a pawn, and has almost no practical chances to trick his opponent. } ) 
Qxc7 29. Nxe6 dxe6 30. Rxd8 $18 { White has 'cashed in' his advantage for one pawn. Coupled with the superior activity this is enough to be objectively winning. As a side note, the failure to find 28.Qa5 would go on to haunt Botvinnik, as he was later tricked and lost the game! It's a good reminder that, when in a winning position, it pays to be ruthless and to snuff out even the smallest of practical chances for your opponent. } 
