 { Since backward pawns and outposts go hand in hand, whenever you have a semi-open file in your favor it's worth thinking of strategies around the outpost available to you. In this position, taken from the game Barden, L - Rossolimo,N (1950) Black has a backward pawn on d6 and so White should consider strategies that target the pawn, or that take advantage of the outpost on d5. However, instead he makes a huge positional mistake on his next move. } 19. { -KEY- } b4 $2 { White needlessly gifts his opponent an outpost on c4, at the same time creating backward pawns on a3 and c2. This reckless move set the scene for an eventual defeat. } Nb6 $1 { Immediately hunting the square. } 20. Bc1 { Retreating to defend the a3 pawn. } ( 20. Bxb6 $2 { would only worsen matters for White since after } Qxb6 { White has too many weaknesses on the dark squares. } ) 
Nc4 21. Nd5 { White jumps into the outpost now, but it only worsens matters. } Bxd5 22. exd5 { The d-file has been shut down, while the c4 outpost remains especially strong since the knight has the backing of the Black rook on c8. } ( 22. Qxd5 Qxd5 23. Rxd5 Bg5 $17 { White would also suffer a lot here, as Black will force off the bishop pair and leave White with holes along both light and dark squares. } ) 
Bg5 $1 { Black's central pawns are on dark squares, so exchanging his bishop is a clever idea. } 23. Rfe1 Qe7 24. Be4 Bxc1 25. Rxc1 h5 $1 { A nasty pawn break. White's position is already critical on the queenside, and now his kingside is about to collapse. } 26. Qg3 hxg4 27. Qxg4 Qf6 28. Bd3 Rh4 { In the end, Black's rook entry comes along the semi-open h-file. The position is already lost. In case you are curious, you can check out the rest of the game unannotated. } 29. Qg3 Nb6 30. Re4 Rh5 31. Rg4 Nxd5 32. Rxg7 Nf4 33. Rg8+ Kd7 34. Rxc8 Kxc8 35. Qg8+ Kb7 36. Kh1 Qh6 37. Qg1 Nxd3 38. cxd3 Rxh2+ ( { White resigned since after } 38... Rxh2+ 38. Qxh2 { there is } Qxc1 { . } ) 

