 { In our final backward pawns example we will explore the link between semi-open files, outposts and backward pawns. The position appeared in the game Carlsen,M - Bacrot,E (2022) . } 12. { -KEY- } Kb1 $1 { Anticipating that the c-file may open up in the near future. } dxc4 $6 { The veteran Grandmaster from France goes for a dubious plan of queenside expansion. } 13. Bxc4 b5 { This was Black's idea. In principle it makes sense to go for a pawn storm since White's king is on the queenside. The problem is that now White has a semi-open c-file to operate on. } 14. Be2 a5 15. g4 { White goes for his own expansion. } O-O 16. g5 Nh5 ( { Black would like to bring the knight to centre, but the problem is that after } 16... Nd5 17. h4 $16 { there is no way for Black to stop the h5 pawn break, when White's attack will prove to be the more powerful one. } ) 
17. Ne4 $1 { With Black's knight stranded on h5, White plants his own on the e4 square. The positional attack along the c-file begins to take shape. } Rfc8 18. Rc1 $1 Bb4 ( 18... c5 $2 { is not possible because of } 19. Bxb5 cxd4 20. Qxc8 Rxc8 21. Rxc8 Kh7 22. exd4 $18 { Black's queen stands no chance against the two rooks and a pawn. } ) 
 ( 18... e5 $6 { only makes matters worse for Black, since after } 19. dxe5 Nxe5 20. f4 Nd7 21. Nxd6 Qxd6 22. Rhd1 $18 { White is left with the bishop pair, a better centre, and tremendous piece pressure along the c- and d-files. } ) 
19. f4 $1 { Stabilising the position, and killing off any possibility of an ...e5 break. } ( { Black's last move was probably played in the hope that White would play } 19. Bxb4 { , when at least after } axb4 { Black would get a semi-open file of his own, keeping alive some faint hopes of drumming up an attack. } ) 
Bxd2 20. Qxd2 { With the exchange of dark-squared bishops Black loses his best defender of the c5 square. } Nb6 21. Nc5 $1 { The start of a precise knight maneuver. } Nd5 22. Nd3 $1 { By the way, this game was played in 3+2 blitz time controls as it was a Titled Tuesday event on chess.com. In spite of the fast time controls, here's a fun fact: Magnus has played the top engine move every time since our starting position! } a4 23. Bf3 Qd6 24. Rc5 { With the knight having left the outpost after 22.Nd3, it's the turn of the rook to occupy it. } Nb6 25. Ne5 { Cutting out ...Nc4 ideas. } Nd5 26. Rhc1 a3 27. b3 { White's strategy comes to a successful end. The backward pawn on c6 is about to fall. } Ne7 ( 27... Ra6 { can be met by } 28. Rxb5 cxb5 29. Rxc8 Kh7 30. Nxf7 $18 ) 
28. Nxc6 Nxc6 29. Rxc6 Rxc6 30. Rxc6 $18 { White crowns his strategy and collects the backward pawn. To make matters worse for Black, his remaining queenside pawns are weak while his knight remains locked out of the action. } Qd7 31. Rc5 e5 $2 { A final blunder in time trouble, though the position was already hopeless. } 32. Bxa8 { White won by resignation. In going for a plan of queenside expansion, Black had to accept a vulnerable pawn along the semi-open c-file. From here, Magnus coordinated his pieces to make sure it was a backward pawn and then applied piece pressure until the pawn could be captured. This brings us to our second topic of the section, where we'll show examples where one side applies piece pressure along a semi-open file. } 
