 { Since in the case of semi-open files there is a power imbalance between each side's heavy pieces, you should be seeking out opportunities to apply piece pressure along the file when possible. Let's take a look at a typical example. } 1... { -KEY- } Qh5 $1 { The threat of pinning the knight is especially strong with the rook x-raying it from f8. Since White cannot untangle, the position is already lost. } 2. Nxf6+ { Exchanging pieces would be a good solution if not for the semi-open file. } ( 2. Be3 { This move protects the d4 square, but the pressure against the f3 knight becomes too great after } Nxe4 3. Qxe4 Bg4 $19 { Black wins at least an exchange. } ) 
Rxf6 $1 { Threatening ...Rxf3 next. } 3. Ng5 { White tries an aggressive solution. } ( 3. Qe4 Bg4 4. Rd3 Raf8 { The arrival of the second rook forces a collapse of the kingside. } 5. Nh4 Rxf2 $19 ) 
Nd4 { The presence of a central outpost compounds White's problems. } 4. Qd3 Bf5 ( 4... Rd6 { , switching to the open file and combining the threats of ...Bxg5 and ...Ne2+, was another way to win. } ) 
5. Qd2 Rh6 $1 { Due to the rook lift to the third rank, the h-file functions as a semi-open file in spite of both sides having a pawn. } 6. Bd5+ Kf8 ( { But not } 6... Kh8 $2 { since then White would have had } 7. Nf7 { . } ) 
7. h4 Bxg5 8. hxg5 { Now mate is unavoidable. } ( { If } 8. Qxg5 { then Black picks up a full rook with } Qxd1 { . } ) 
Qh2+ 9. Kf1 Bh3+ 10. Ke1 Qg1# { We saw in this example how semi-open files are extremely dangerous dynamic avenues. Since this is a positional course, we won't look at any more examples of aggression along the semi-open file. Instead, we'll finish the section by covering a couple of common patterns to illustrate how you can ease the pressure as the defending side. } 
