 { This position was reached in an online game between Magnus Carlsen and GM Aleksei Sarana. White has a semi-open d-file, but Black's d6 pawn is for now very stable. Because of this, Magnus focuses on managing the pressure against him on the c-file. } 19. { -KEY- } Qe1 $1 { Prophylaxis against the ...d5 break, and also lending some stability to the knight on a5 by x-raying it. } ( 19. a3 $2 { One careless mistake could cost White the game. } d5 $1 { Whenever fighting along semi-open files, you have to be extremely careful of tactical pawn breaks. If one side can carry out a pawn break like this, they will almost always be better or even winning, since they are able to get rid of their main positional problem - the backward pawn. } 20. Qf3 $2 { White can sidestep the pin, but this allows Black to set up the dream structure. } ( 20. exd5 { was the lesser evil, but after } Bc5 $17 { Black is winning material. } ) 
d4 $1 { The d6 pawn has gone from Black's major weakness to his biggest strength. } 21. Na2 Nc4 22. Nxc4 bxc4 $19 { Improving the structure. It's true that the c-file is no longer semi-open. However, the b-file has opened up in its place, and this one is even stronger given that the b2 focal point is on a dark square. This favors Black as they are the only side with the dark-squared bishop. White is already lost as he has no good reply against the threat of the ...c3 pawn break. } ) 
Qc7 { Black applies piece pressure along the semi-open file and once again forces White to find the most precise defense. } 20. Rg2 $1 { The only move that keeps an advantage. When facing pressure along a semi-open file, it is not uncommon that the path to an advantage will be very narrow. This is because usually the opponent will have a lot of dynamic possibilities available to them, like a series of landmines that you must make sure not to step on. } ( 20. a3 $6 Na4 $1 { and now if Nxa4 then Black can reply with ...Qxc2+. } ) 
 ( 20. Rd2 $2 { This clunky arrangement allows Black to get the ideal pawn break on the semi-open file after } b4 21. Nd1 d5 $1 { Black stands much better, since White cannot play } 22. exd5 Nxd5 23. Rxd5 { due to } Qxc2 24. Ka1 Qc1 ) 
 ( 20. Qe2 $2 { White should not forget the benefit of having his queen on e1 x-raying the a5 knight, since here after } d5 $1 21. exd5 { Necessary to avoid losing a piece. } ( 21. Nxd5 $2 Nxd5 22. Rxd5 Qxa5 $19 { Black picks up the undefended knight. } ) 
Bb4 $1 22. Nc6 Bxc3 23. bxc3 Qd6 $17 { Black stands much better due to White's ruined structure. In particular the outposts on c4 and a4 are dangerous as they can assist a quick mating attack. } ) 
Rfe8 ( 20... b4 $1 { It was best to play this while it was still possible, though after } 21. Nd5 Nxd5 22. Rxd5 $16 { White has a nice edge, since their c2 pawn is well-guarded while Black's d6 pawn is a long-term problem. } ) 
21. a3 $1 { Now is a good time to shut down ...b4 advances. } Bd8 22. Nb3 { The knight has done its job of guarding against ...Nc4 jumps. } Nc4 23. Nd5 $1 { Magnus spotted that if Black does get tempted by ...Nc4 jumps, then he will abandon control over d5. } Qb7 24. g4 { With the pressure largely resolved on the queenside, the time is right to make further progress on the kingside. } a5 25. g5 a4 ( { The } 25... b4 { break has no sting, since White can reply with } 26. a4 $18 ) 
26. Nc1 b4 { Desperation. Black knows he's positionally lost on the kingside, so he gives up a pawn to try to open lines. } 27. axb4 Bb6 28. Na2 a3 29. b3 $1 { The lines stay closed! } Nb2 30. Rdd2 Bd4 31. c3 { White has nothing short of a pawn bunker on the queenside! Black had seen enough, and resigned. This game was masterful play from Carlsen, who showed a deep understanding of how to manage piece pressure along the semi-open file. Themes like placing a knight on a5, queen on e1, and guarding against the ...d5 pawn break are established patterns in these deeply studied Sicilian structures. } 
