 { In the first example we saw how Magnus used various defensive techniques to hold the base pawn on c2. In this case, the piece pressure along the semi-open file is mostly on the c3 and c4 squares. White has to worry about several annoying ideas, like ...Nc4, ...Qb4, or even the exchange sacrifice with...Rxc3. Fortunately, White can rely on a strong sequence of moves that will completely neutralise the pressure and leave them with a stable edge. Can you find it? } 1. { -KEY- } Nd5 $1 ( 1. f3 $6 { White's advantage would quickly disappear with a typical move like this one: } Qb4 2. Rb1 Nc4 3. Bxc4 Qxc4 { The pressure forces White to give up the bishop pair. } 4. Rfd1 b5 $15 { Black stands better with active pieces and continued pressure along the c-file. } ) 
Qd8 ( 1... Qxd2 $6 { The tactical complications do not favor Black. } 2. Nxe7 Kh8 3. Rxd2 Nxe4 4. Nxc8 Nxd2 5. Bxd2 Rxc8 6. c3 $16 { White has a great endgame, with the bishop pair and a better structure. } ) 
2. Nxf6+ Bxf6 3. b3 $14 { Correct! The final move in the sequence is important, to kill off the last remaining threat of ...Nc4. Next up White can even play c2-c4, establishing a Maroczy bind. White stands better with more central space and the bishop pair. Some of you will have seen this pattern before. Much like the defensive configuration that Magnus went for in our previous example, the idea of exchanging one pair of knights and fortifying the structure with b3 and c4 has been known for many years. This brings us to a close for this section, and indeed for the chapter on the whole. What's more, it concludes the part of the course that focuses on piece play. From here, we'll turn our attention to Pawn Play, before finishing the course with a chapter dedicated to the endgame. But before this, feel free to review the chapter summary and - importantly - good luck in the exercise set. Since this was a shorter chapter, there are fewer puzzles to solve than usual. Plus, perhaps a lighter section is deserved after the big puzzle set of the previous chapter! } 
